WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.970
- Trump's secret documents
versus Hillary's emails.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.803
Lock her up.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.100
No, lock him up.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.290
Lock them up?

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.540
Former President Trump has
been fighting to contain

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.400
and fundraise from the political fallout

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.930
after the FBI executed a search warrant

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.760
at his residence at Mar-a-Lago.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.050
We have since learned that agents sought

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.390
to recover classified
documents being stored on site,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.220
including confidential documents relating

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.020
to nuclear weapons or energy.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.350
Among the items seized by FBI
agents were at least 11 sets

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.720
of classified documents,
four marked top secret,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.880
three marked as secret, and
three marked as confidential,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.340
the lowest level of
classified information.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.800
After the warrant was unsealed,
or at least parts of it,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.050
we learned that the FBI is
investigating the former

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.970
president for potential
criminal violations

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.950
of the Espionage Act, obstruction

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.840
and destruction of records.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.090
- Were they looking for
the Hillary Clinton emails

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.520
that were deleted, but
they are around someplace?

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.680
- In response, several of
the ex-presidents defenders

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.080
in Congress have seamlessly
flipped from back the blue

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.060
to defund the FBI.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.670
Other allies have opted to
invoke their favorite defensive,

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.130
all things Trump, but her emails,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.020
and by her, I mean Hillary Clinton,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.090
whose use of a private email server

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.740
while serving as Secretary of State,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.380
resulted in multiple
investigations into her handling

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.970
of classified information,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.190
and many of Trump's defenders have claimed

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.080
that he is a target of a double standard

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.140
and a political witch hunt
compared to Hillary Clinton.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.660
Senator Lindsay Graham even
went on to Fox News to suggest

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.360
that prosecuting Trump while
not prosecuting Clinton

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.190
would result in massive rioting.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.260
- If there's a prosecution of Donald Trump

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.840
for mishandling classified information

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.540
after the Clinton debacle,
which you presided over

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.070
and did a hell of a good job,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.930
there'll be riots in the streets.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.930
- Now at least facially, this
comparison doesn't seem crazy.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.940
So let's compare the investigations

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.980
into Hillary Clinton's
private email server

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.770
with the FBI's seizure of
Donald Trump's top secret

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.570
documents at Mar-a-Lago and address

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.820
whether Clinton received
preferential treatment compared

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.620
to the former president
when it comes to handling

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:50.820
of classified material.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.800
So what was that whole scandal about?

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.750
Well, in January, 2009, Clinton aids set

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.490
up an email domain called Clintonemail.com

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.810
on a private server located at
her Chappaqua, New York home.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.130
While running the State Department,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.440
Clinton exclusively used
email addresses connected

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.890
to that private server for
both work and personal emails,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.890
rather than using a state.gov
email for government work

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.440
and a separate private account
for her personal affairs.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.690
In May of 2014, the House
of Representatives voted

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.800
to create a select committee

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.080
to investigate the 2012 terrorist attack

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.360
on government facilities
in Benghazi, Libya

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.440
that killed four Americans,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.130
including Ambassador, Chris
Stevens, and on March 2nd, 2015,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.860
Clinton's private email
server became a national story

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.780
when the New York Times
ran a front page article

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.620
on the subject.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.007
The article detailed
that the system, quote,

00:02:33.007 --> 00:02:34.890
"may have violated federal requirements,"

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.990
and was characterized
as alarming by current

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.060
and former government archive officials.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.460
Clinton claims she set up
her own email for convenience

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.770
so she would only need
to carry one smartphone

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.320
with one email rather
than two devices for work

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.640
and personal affairs,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.743
but skeptics believe the
more likely explanation was

00:02:49.743 --> 00:02:52.590
that Clinton, who was gearing
up to run for president,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.260
wanted to be the sole arbiter
of what emails were personal

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.000
and what emails were government business

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.310
that needed to be turned
over in response to freedom

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.620
of information requests and
congressional inquiries,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.510
and Clinton's team did decide on their own

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.120
what emails needed to be
preserved and which did not,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.120
igniting a firestorm from
critics that exist to this day,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.580
and on March 10th, 2015,
Clinton's office disclosed

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.820
that she kept 30,490 work related emails

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.360
and deleted approximately 31,830 emails

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.650
that she had deemed personal,

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.050
which she described as
communications about private matters

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.150
like yoga routines,
her daughter's wedding,

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.230
and her mother's funeral.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.360
However, it turns out those
emails had only been intended

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.520
to have been deleted and had
not actually been deleted

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.620
until three weeks after
Congress subpoenaed Clinton's

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.130
Benghazi related emails,
and back in December, 2014,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.140
Clinton's former Chief
of Staff, Cheryl Mills,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.820
had told Plat River Networks,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.680
the company that was
managing the private server,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.387
that Clinton, quote, "decided
she no longer needed access

00:03:43.387 --> 00:03:46.260
"to any of her emails older than 60 days."

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.870
Mills instructed an
unidentified PRN employee

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:52.350
to modify the email retention
policy to reflect this change.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.080
However, the PRN employee
mistakenly did not implement

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.850
this change and did not
delete the old emails

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:57.960
when requested.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.320
Sometime between March
25th and 31st, 2015,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.620
this employee erased the emails
using a free utility program

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.520
called BleachBit.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.410
Clinton later told the
FBI that she was not aware

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.900
that the emails were
deleted in late March, 2015,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.850
and a Clinton campaign spokesman claimed

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.550
that the PRN employee was
advised to retain all data

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.590
in compliance with the
congressional subpoena

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:20.400
but acted on his own to complete
the prior deletion request.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.890
In September, 2015, investigators
with the FBI were able

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.050
to recover some of the deleted emails.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.510
Clinton's critics have
accused her and her aids

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.370
of deleting emails that
were under subpoena

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.840
to cover up wrongdoing.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.000
Of course, during the
campaign, chants of lock her up

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.770
were regular features at Trump rallies

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.870
while Trump, himself,
famously invited Russia

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.960
to hack Hillary Clinton's server
to find the deleted emails.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.150
- Russia, if you're listening,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:47.150
I hope you are able to
find the 30,000 emails

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.780
that are missing.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.920
I think you will probably be
rewarded mightily by our press.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.840
- But in July, 2015, Inspectors General

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.310
at the State Department and the Director

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.650
of National Intelligence
asked the DOJ to review

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.420
whether classified
information was compromised

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.520
by Clinton's use of private email.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.260
The following month, it
was confirmed the FBI

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.640
was investigating whether Clinton

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.250
or her staff violated federal
laws governing classified

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.170
information and whether foreign powers

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:13.170
or hostile actors had
hacked the private server.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.080
The FBI investigated Clinton
under three criminal statutes,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.020
18 U.S.C. 793, which makes it a felony

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.600
to mishandled classified
information in an intentional

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.450
or a grossly negligent
way, 18 U.S.C. 1924,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.670
which makes it a misdemeanor
to knowingly remove classified

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.310
information from appropriate
systems or storage facilities,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.850
and 18 U.S.C. 2071, which
makes it illegal to willfully

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.760
and unlawfully conceal, remove,
or destroy federal records,

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.580
and on July 5th, 2016,
FBI Director, James Comey,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.620
held a press conference
announcing the department's

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.570
conclusion that no reasonable prosecutor

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.850
would bring a criminal case
against the former Secretary

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.370
of State due to lack of
requisite criminal intent.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.830
Nevertheless, Comey also
famously remarked that Clinton

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.787
and her aids were, quote,
"extremely careless

00:05:51.787 --> 00:05:53.347
"in their handling of very sensitive,

00:05:53.347 --> 00:05:55.200
"highly classified information,"

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.430
and on October 28th, 2016, 11
days before the election day,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.680
Comey announced the FBI
was reviewing new emails

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.960
that could be relevant to the
probe of Clinton's server.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.610
The emails were being examined as part

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:06.780
of the FBI's investigation

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.420
into disgraced former
Congressman, Anthony Wiener,

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.451
who was the estranged
husband of Top Clinton aid,

00:06:12.451 --> 00:06:14.160
Huma Abedin.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.220
In a November 6th, 2016
letter to lawmakers,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.250
Comey announced his agency's
review of emails was complete,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.192
and it did not alter
the previous conclusion

00:06:22.192 --> 00:06:24.420
that Clinton should not be prosecuted.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.730
Two days later, Clinton was
defeated by Donald Trump

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.110
in the presidential election,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.480
and while there are conflicting
accounts of the emails

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.270
at issue, it seems that of the
tens of thousands of emails

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.880
that were reviewed, 113 were found

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.550
to contain classified information
and only three of those

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.200
had classification markers,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.120
65 of those emails were deemed secret,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.220
and 22 deemed top secret.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.490
In 2016, Comey said that Clinton
should have known that some

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.700
of those 113 were classified
but noted that there

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.760
were others that she might
have understandably missed.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.670
On June 14th, 2018, the DOJs office

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.490
of Inspector General
released a report on the FBI

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.460
and DOJ's handling of the
Clinton investigation.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.830
The report found no
evidence of political bias

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.260
and reaffirmed the decision
not to prosecute Clinton,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.790
and in September, 2019,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.280
a three year State Department
investigation concluded

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.310
that 38 current and former
State Department officials

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.490
were culpable in 91 cases of
sending classified information

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.860
that ended up in Clinton's email server.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.320
Though the report stated that
the private email increased

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.300
the vulnerability of
classified information,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.007
investigators found, quote,
"No persuasive evidence

00:07:27.007 --> 00:07:29.257
"of systemic deliberate mishandling

00:07:29.257 --> 00:07:30.630
"of classified information."

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.820
But let's compare that
to what we know right now

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.360
about the Trump Mar-a-Lago
scandal, which, admittedly,

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.750
is absolutely not complete
information at this time,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.910
but after the FBI
executed a search warrant

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.070
on Donald Trump's Mar-a-Lago estate,

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:46.020
the former president posted a statement

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.480
on a social media platform,
Truth Social, attacking,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.537
what he called the, quote,

00:07:49.537 --> 00:07:51.750
"weaponization of the justice system,"

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:53.317
calling the search, "political targeting

00:07:53.317 --> 00:07:54.540
"at the highest level."

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.310
Trump claimed that there
was a double standard with

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.980
how the FBI handled Hillary
Clinton's email investigation,

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.117
quote, "Hillary Clinton
was allowed to delete

00:08:01.117 --> 00:08:04.777
"and acid wash 33,000 emails
after they were subpoenaed

00:08:04.777 --> 00:08:05.610
"by Congress.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.220
"Absolutely nothing has happened
to hold her accountable."

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.830
Now, putting aside that Clinton
did not literally cover the

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.320
server in bleach, obviously,
you take that internally

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:16.860
to fight covid, Trump and his
allies have long suggested

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.810
that Hillary Clinton should
have been prosecuted,

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.170
or at least, at a minimum,
prosecuting Trump would be unfair

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.900
of the double standard
created for Clinton,

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.130
but the use of private emails

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:27.450
and other unofficial communications

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.820
for government employees
has come up more often

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.730
than you'd think with varying
degrees of uproar and concern,

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.190
and though that certainly doesn't
excuse Clinton's behavior,

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.420
it's probably important to look

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.970
at other context and comparisons.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.340
Among those who recently
used private email accounts

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:42.720
to conduct official government business

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.600
in the Trump administration,
that includes Advisor,

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.240
Steven Miller, former Chief
of Staff, Reince Preibus,

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.150
former National Economic
Council Director, Gary Cohn,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.220
former Deputy National Security Advisor,

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.710
K. T. McFarland, and former
Advisor, Steve Bannon.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:57.540
Trump's son-in-law, Jared
Kushner, and daughter,

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.730
Ivanka Trump, both presidential advisors,

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.347
used either personal email
accounts or the messaging app,

00:09:02.347 --> 00:09:05.040
WhatsApp, to conduct official business,

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.960
and since WhatsApp messages are encrypted,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.030
there's no record of
the message unless Jared

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.090
and Ivanka actually stored
copies of those messages.

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.777
Kushner's lawyer said his client, quote,

00:09:13.777 --> 00:09:15.667
"took images of
communications from WhatsApp

00:09:15.667 --> 00:09:17.640
"and forwarded them to official accounts,"

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.560
but when asked if Kushner used the app

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.390
to share classified information,

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.987
his attorney diverted saying, quote,

00:09:22.987 --> 00:09:25.200
"That's above my pay grade."

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.480
Former White House Trade
Advisor, Peter Navarro,

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.940
also fighting contempt charges
for refusing to testify

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.400
to the January 6th committee,
is currently embroiled

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.050
in his own presidential record dispute

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.610
with the National Archives.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.970
In August, 2022, the DOJ sued
Navarro claiming he refused

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.100
to turn over emails retained
on a private account

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.110
while working for the
Trump administration,

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.570
and even Colin Powell, who
served as Secretary of State

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:48.570
under George W. Bush from 2001 to 2005,

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.850
acknowledged using a
laptop on a private line

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.160
for State Department business
including sending notes

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.830
to ambassadors and foreign
ministers via personal email,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.400
and in a January, 2009 email
exchange with Hillary Clinton,

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.560
Powell told incoming Secretary of State

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.930
that he used a private phone
and computer to do business

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.640
with foreign leaders and communicate

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:06.750
with senior department officials

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.850
on their own personal email accounts.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:10.890
While Powell said that
Clinton was not influenced

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.470
by his email with
respect to setting her up

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.727
on her own server, he
also said he was, quote,

00:10:15.727 --> 00:10:17.587
"not aware at the time of any requirement

00:10:17.587 --> 00:10:19.147
"for private unclassified exchanges

00:10:19.147 --> 00:10:21.600
"to be treated as official records."

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.540
But again, to emphasize, this is all bad.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.270
It's a problem in Washington,
whether it's Hillary Clinton

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:28.440
or anyone else doing it.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.240
Now, before we get to
the legal consequences,

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.910
let's compare and contrast some
of the factual differences,

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.440
because some of them just jump right out.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:37.050
First of all, at the time
of this particular scandal,

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.850
Hillary Clinton was a government employee.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.920
In fact, she was Secretary of State,

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.860
just a few rungs below the
president of the United States.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.510
She was conducting government business

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:46.770
at the highest levels.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.780
She had an active security clearance,

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.060
and she had communications
with other people

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.260
who had security clearances to
view the classified documents

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.730
and classified information,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.260
and she was conducting government business

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.300
on a private server, which she absolutely

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.030
should not have been doing,
and ironically, was a violation

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:05.460
of the PRA, the Presidential Records Act,

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:06.390
and while there's no doubt

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.150
that classified material got
mixed in, by all accounts,

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.370
this was an inadvertent mishandling.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.130
In contrast, Donald Trump is not currently

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:15.300
a government employee.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.060
He doesn't have any
security clearances at all.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:19.869
He was not communicating with people

00:11:19.869 --> 00:11:21.450
with security clearances.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.810
In fact, we don't know who
had access to these documents.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.390
Odds are none of these
people had the kinds

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.360
of clearances required, and we don't know

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.970
to whom these documents were transmitted

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.430
or had access to them,
and while it's possible

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.440
that Trump's possession of
these government documents

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.330
started out as an inadvertent,

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:42.270
it appears clear that, at
some point down the chain,

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:45.750
it was intentional and conscious
to withhold these documents

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.760
from being returned to the government.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.370
There's also a volume
in number difference.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.590
In Trump's case, we're
talking about hundreds

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.620
of classified documents,
potentially thousands or tens

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.440
of thousands of pages
of classified material.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.710
We can tell from the photographs
that many, if not all,

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:03.990
of these documents are
clearly marked as classified

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.050
or top secret versus a
comparatively small amount,

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:10.260
in the case of Hillary
Clinton, you have 113 emails

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.720
that contain classified information,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.480
only three of which were
marked as confidential,

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.760
and while it wouldn't change
the underlying culpability,

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:18.750
there was a big difference in

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.178
how the situation was
handled once discovered.

00:12:21.178 --> 00:12:24.600
Hillary Clinton cooperated
with the DOJs investigation.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.520
It doesn't appear that Hillary
Clinton ever represented

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.340
that everything had been turned
over when it clearly hadn't.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.830
In comparison, Donald Trump, it seems,

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:35.280
consciously hid the documents,
refused to turn it over,

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.260
and lied to investigators,

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.060
but just because the situation
is different doesn't mean

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.280
that Hillary Clinton should
not have been prosecuted.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:42.690
So let's talk about the charge

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.910
that she received preferential treatment.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:48.000
On July 5th, 2016, FBI
Director, James Comey,

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:48.930
held a press conference

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:50.490
where he made the following statement.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:52.980
- Although we did not find clear evidence

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.800
that Secretary Clinton or
her colleagues intended

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:57.960
to violate laws governing the handling

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.720
of classified information,
there is evidence

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.960
that they were extremely
careless in their handling

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.600
of very sensitive, highly
classified information.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.820
- So was Comey's statement
that no reasonable prosecutor

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.790
would bring a case correct or
evidence of political bias?

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:14.550
Well, in 2016, the FBI told
the Senate Judiciary Committee

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.110
that, quote, "extremely careless,"

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:18.750
was Comey's common sense way
of describing the actions

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.910
of Clinton and her colleagues
and was not intended

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.280
to describe the legal
standard for gross negligence

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:24.690
under the Espionage Act,

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.310
but Trump and his supporters have argued

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:27.990
if Clinton was indeed extremely careless

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.810
with the classified materials,
the failure to prosecute her,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.930
at least for grossly
mishandling classified material

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:36.780
under the Espionage Act, was
a miscarriage of justice,

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:38.010
but it's not enough to
look at the statute.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.290
We have to look at how
prosecutors have brought charges

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.430
under this section.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:44.310
Now back in 2016, Andrew
Torrez, the lawyer

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.440
and host of the podcast,
Opening Arguments,

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.050
which is excellent,
researched all prosecutions

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.080
under the Espionage Act,
and out of 240 cases,

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.450
Torrez only found nine
cases that have been brought

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.880
under the gross negligence
part of the statute,

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:59.340
and in all of those
gross negligence cases,

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:01.057
the defendant was found
to have made, quote,

00:14:01.057 --> 00:14:03.847
"a deliberate decision to
hide or destroy information

00:14:03.847 --> 00:14:06.577
"that the defendant knew to
be confidential and improperly

00:14:06.577 --> 00:14:07.890
"in his possession."

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.540
Torrez concluded that Comey's statement

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.190
that no reasonable
prosecutor would bring a case

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:13.447
against Clinton was
correct, write-in quote,

00:14:13.447 --> 00:14:16.507
"I can find no case anywhere
in which any person has ever

00:14:16.507 --> 00:14:18.997
"been sentenced under the
Espionage Act without evidence

00:14:18.997 --> 00:14:21.127
"that said person
unambiguously knew the material

00:14:21.127 --> 00:14:23.287
"that they were removing was confidential.

00:14:23.287 --> 00:14:24.817
"Since that fact is not present here,

00:14:24.817 --> 00:14:26.707
"I conclude that Hillary
Clinton received no special

00:14:26.707 --> 00:14:28.950
"treatment when the government
declined to indict her."

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.680
- We're talking about 18 U.S.C. 793.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:32.970
That's the Espionage Act.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.690
It is the same law, with some
amendments, that was used

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.240
to prosecute Julius and Ethel Rosenberg.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.668
Virtually all of the
prosecutions under it are for

00:14:42.668 --> 00:14:45.728
what we typically think of as spy stuff,

00:14:45.728 --> 00:14:48.420
someone with access to
documents that relate

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.330
to the national defense,
steals or makes a copy

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:54.360
of those documents, and prosecutors
don't even have to prove

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.820
that person ever sold it
to a hostile foreign power

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:58.020
or another spy.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.110
You just have to prove that
they took the document.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.450
You could infer that they
weren't gonna do anything good

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:06.210
with it, and while espionage
no longer carries the death

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:10.200
penalty, it is a felony with
a 10 year maximum sentence,

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:14.100
and that's why we never find any cases

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.030
that are even a close analog
to Secretary Clinton's conduct.

00:15:18.030 --> 00:15:19.590
It isn't espionage.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:23.250
When someone leaves a file
cabinet or a door unlocked

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:26.040
or leaves a top secret
document out on their desk.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:28.140
That's certainly extremely careless,

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.870
but those cases aren't
considered espionage,

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:34.680
and you probably noticed I used
the weasel word, virtually.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:37.980
Virtually all the prosecutions
under the Espionage Act are

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:40.200
for spy stuff, but what about the ones,

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.580
the handful, that aren't?

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.530
Consider US v Gonzalez.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.720
This involved an Air Force staff sergeant,

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.540
who was stationed in Anchorage,
Alaska and was taken off

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.120
to go party with his buddy,
who worked for an oil company.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.510
Sarge grabbed a bunch of his
stuff to give to his buddy

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.400
and while doing so,

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:59.040
accidentally grabbed two
classified documents.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.680
When he got to Prudho Bay,
he discovered his mistake,

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:04.890
but instead of calling his
superior and owning up to it,

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.957
he told his buddy, "Hey,
man, I'm just gonna stick

00:16:07.957 --> 00:16:09.787
"these two documents in your desk,

00:16:09.787 --> 00:16:13.747
"and totally, make sure you
remind me before I leave

00:16:13.747 --> 00:16:16.530
"to take them back with me
to the Air Force base, okay?"

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.260
For whatever reason, use your imagination,

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:23.790
the Sarge and his buddy
totally spaced on the documents

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:27.001
and just left them there in the desk.

00:16:27.001 --> 00:16:29.430
That might not have been such a big deal

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:33.510
except that the buddy didn't
actually have his own desk.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.310
He shared it with another guy named Nick,

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:37.890
and Nick got to work Monday morning,

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:39.090
opened up his desk drawer,

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.960
saw the bright red confidential
sticker on the cover sheet

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.350
of the two documents, and
freaked out, as you do.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:49.020
He called his boss, his
boss called the Air Force,

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.260
and one thing led to
another, Sarge was sentenced

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:56.520
to 43 days of confinement, a
little less than the 10 years,

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:59.910
discharged for bad
conduct, reduced in rank,

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.810
or consider US v Gaffney,
which coincidentally involved

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:06.120
another Air Force staff
sergeant, although, this guy

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:08.910
would never ever trek
off to Northern Alaska

00:17:08.910 --> 00:17:10.770
for a weekend long bender.

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.080
No, this guy was a good family man.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:15.660
He just wanted to spend more time at home

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:18.450
with his pregnant wife, and so he did,

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.120
but one of sarges job duties was

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:26.120
to shred classify documents,
and being that this was 1982,

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.920
shredders were super slow.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.590
Rather than spending
hours shredding documents

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.380
like a dumbbell, he did what anybody

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:37.380
with a pregnant wife might
do, grabbed all the documents,

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:41.400
then deposited them in a nearby
dumpster, problem solved,

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.240
but except that helicopter
parenting wouldn't be invented

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.170
for two decades.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:49.410
So kids played in dumpsters all the time.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:53.340
Some rugrat found the
documents, took them home,

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:56.610
daddy found them under the bed,
hey, could have been worse,

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:57.870
and called the Air Force.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.660
All of these cases that don't quite fit

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:03.450
into the spy stuff category are like this.

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:07.050
They're cases in which the
defendant absolutely knew

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:10.740
that they had compromised
documents in their possession

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.590
and then they still did the stupid thing,

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:16.050
and then they tried to
actively cover their tracks.

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:19.537
As Jim Comey put it, "All of
the cases prosecuted involve

00:18:19.537 --> 00:18:22.387
"some combination of clearly intentional

00:18:22.387 --> 00:18:25.680
"and willful mishandling
of classified information,"

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:28.410
the very behavior for
which Secretary Clinton

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:29.370
was exonerated.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:32.430
- But look, don't take
legal advice from a podcast.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.920
Take legal advice from a YouTube channel,

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:37.500
but why would prosecutors
only bring cases involving

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.180
deliberate conduct when
the statute requires

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:40.620
only gross negligence?

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.420
Well, that's because the
gross negligent statute

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.310
might be unconstitutionally vague.

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.740
Under the vagueness doctrine,
statutes that are vague,

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.780
indefinite, or uncertain
violate due process,

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:51.600
because such laws fail to give
the individual proper notice

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.910
of how to conform their
conduct to follow the law,

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:56.160
and in a case called Gorin
versus the United States,

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:57.420
the Supreme Court suggested

00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:59.070
that the then operative provisions

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.890
that the Espionage Act was
not unconstitutionally vague,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.717
only because it criminalized
intentional conduct, quote,

00:19:04.717 --> 00:19:06.997
"The obvious delimiting words
in the statute are those

00:19:06.997 --> 00:19:10.207
"requiring intent or reason to
believe that the information

00:19:10.207 --> 00:19:12.577
"to be obtained is to
be used to the injury

00:19:12.577 --> 00:19:15.037
"of the United States, which
requires those prosecuted

00:19:15.037 --> 00:19:16.920
"to have acted in bad faith."

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.920
And in an August 16th, 2016
letter explaining the decision

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:21.330
not to bring the charges,

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:23.190
the FBI told the Senate
Judiciary Committee

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.860
that the DOJ had only prosecuted
for intentional mishandling

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:28.320
of documents, because the
gross negligence language

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:29.790
is likely unconstitutional.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:31.717
They said, quote, "There are concerns

00:19:31.717 --> 00:19:33.367
"about the constitutional implications

00:19:33.367 --> 00:19:35.887
"of criminalizing
grossly negligent conduct

00:19:35.887 --> 00:19:37.507
"without requiring the government to prove

00:19:37.507 --> 00:19:40.320
"that the person knew he or
she was doing something wrong."

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:43.230
The letter noted that only
one person in 99 years

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:45.450
had been charged with
mishandling classified documents

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:48.390
in a grossly negligent way,
a charge that was dismissed

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:49.620
when the defendant pleaded guilty

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.060
to making false statements.

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.250
So given the potential unconstitutionality

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:56.310
of gross negligence
prosecutions, the DOJ has opted

00:19:56.310 --> 00:19:58.980
to almost exclusively
prosecute cases involving

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.160
only intentional misconduct,
but is Trump subject

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.470
to an unfair double
standard by law enforcement?

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:07.110
Well, so far, legal experts
are pretty much unanimous

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.300
that Trump's case is much, much worse,

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:11.820
and it's not subject to a double standard.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:14.730
As more information about the
FBI search has been released,

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.140
legal experts have weighed in

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:17.970
on whether Trump has been treated unfairly

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:19.860
when compared to Clinton.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.020
David Laufman, who headed
the Justice Department's

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.270
counterintelligence section until 2018,

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:26.760
said there's simply no comparison
between Clinton's emails

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:29.227
and the judge approved
search of Mar-a-Lago, quote,

00:20:29.227 --> 00:20:30.667
"People sling these cases around

00:20:30.667 --> 00:20:32.077
"to suit their political agenda,

00:20:32.077 --> 00:20:35.167
"but every case has to stand
on its own circumstances.

00:20:35.167 --> 00:20:36.817
"For the department to
pursue a search warrant

00:20:36.817 --> 00:20:39.397
"in Mar-a-Lago tells me
that the quantum and quality

00:20:39.397 --> 00:20:41.947
"of the evidence they were
reciting in a search warrant,

00:20:41.947 --> 00:20:44.707
"an affidavit that an FBI agent swore to,

00:20:44.707 --> 00:20:46.807
"was likely so pulverizing in its force

00:20:46.807 --> 00:20:49.027
"as to eviscerate any notion
that the search warrant

00:20:49.027 --> 00:20:51.450
"and this investigation
is politically motivated."

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.940
And Laufman has experience in this area.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:56.070
Not only did he personally
oversee the investigation

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:58.230
into Hillary Clinton's emails,

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:01.290
he also managed the investigation
into David Petraeus,

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:02.700
and here's a quick summary of what led

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:04.080
to the FBI getting a search warrant

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.600
to recover classified documents
and presidential records

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.820
from Trump's residence at Mar-a-Lago.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:12.150
When Trump left office in
January, 2021 boxes of records

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:14.430
that his White House counsel
said should have gone

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.020
to the National Archives
were, instead, shipped

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.730
to Trump's residence at Mar-a-Lago.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.220
Officials at the National
Archives spent eight months

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.920
in negotiations with Trump
to return the documents.

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.980
15 documents were
returned in January, 2022.

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:27.240
Upon reviewing the documents, though,

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:30.300
NARA saw documents clearly
marked classified intermingled

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:33.570
with non-classified info
resulting in a referral to the DOJ

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:36.600
to investigate possible
mishandling of classified material.

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.090
The FBI had evidence that
dozens of additional boxes

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:41.820
with classified info were
still being kept at Mar-a-Lago.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:45.390
So the DOJ obtained a grand
jury subpoena seeking documents

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:46.710
bearing classification markings

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:48.330
that were still in Trump's possession.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:50.700
On June 3rd, Trump's
custodian of records turned

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:52.950
over additional boxes to the DOJ,

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.650
then signed a certification
swearing that a diligence search

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:59.070
had been done and all
responsive documents produced.

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.440
Trump's lawyer told the
FBI agents that all boxes

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:02.970
of classified records had been kept

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:04.620
in a secure storage room.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:07.130
However, the FBI uncovered
multiple sources of evidence

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:09.810
that the response to the grand
jury subpoena was incomplete

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.060
and the classified documents
were still on site.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:13.627
The FBI also had evidence that, quote,

00:22:13.627 --> 00:22:16.117
"government records were
likely concealed and removed

00:22:16.117 --> 00:22:19.567
"from the storage room and
that efforts were likely taken

00:22:19.567 --> 00:22:21.630
"to obstruct the
government's investigation."

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:24.330
So it's against this backdrop
over a year of delays

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:27.390
in returning all classified
documents voluntarily not

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:29.400
turning over documents in
response to the subpoena

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.680
and evidence of obstruction of justice

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:33.510
that the government finally
got a search warrant

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.850
for Mar-a-Lago and conducted the search.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:39.120
Of course, we got more
information on August 18th, 2022

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.320
when Judge Bruce Reinhardt,

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.450
the magistrate overseeing
the Mar-a-Lago search,

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:45.150
unsealed the warrant cover
sheet revealing the FBI was

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:49.110
investigating potential
violations of 18 U.S.C. 793,

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:51.630
willful retention of
National Defense Information,

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:54.390
18 U.S.C. 2071, concealment or removal

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:57.510
of government records, and 18 U.S.C. 1519,

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.910
obstruction of a federal investigation.

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:03.810
Clinton was also investigated
for violating Section 2071,

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.460
but Bradley Moss, a well-respected lawyer

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:07.213
who handles national security cases,

00:23:07.213 --> 00:23:09.510
said that that cases of Clinton

00:23:09.510 --> 00:23:11.760
and Trump are significantly
different given Trump's willful

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:14.047
retention of classified documents, quote,

00:23:14.047 --> 00:23:17.047
"Trump took properly marked
hard copy classified documents

00:23:17.047 --> 00:23:19.147
"from the White House,
shipped them to Florida,

00:23:19.147 --> 00:23:22.260
"and stored them in an unsecured
location at his residence."

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:24.240
Moss also noted that,
while some Trump documents

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:25.710
were clearly marked as classified,

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:27.510
the presence of classified information

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.007
in Clinton's emails was
less obvious, quote,

00:23:30.007 --> 00:23:31.807
"The emails were never
marked as classified,

00:23:31.807 --> 00:23:33.247
"because there were communications

00:23:33.247 --> 00:23:35.520
"from unclassified government accounts."

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:38.310
And similarly, the FBI's August,
2016 letter justifying its

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:39.870
decision not to charge Clinton.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.240
The bureau noted how past
prosecutions involved some

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:46.380
combination of clearly intentional
and willful mishandling

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:48.990
of classified info, significant quantities

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:51.690
of classified info exposed
to support an inference

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.240
of intent, or indications of disloyalty

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:56.400
to the United States,
none of which were present

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.390
with Clinton.

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:59.550
As examples, the FBI
cited past prosecutions

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:01.590
of ex-CIA Director, David Petreius,

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.720
who knowingly exposed
significant classified materials

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:05.130
to his biographer turned mistress

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:06.990
and then lied about it when confronted.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:09.810
Former Bill Clinton National
Security Advisor, Sandy Berger,

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:12.000
who stole clearly marked
classified documents

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.700
from the National Archives
by hiding them in his clothes

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.730
and Afghanistan Naval
Reservist, Bryan Nishimura,

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:20.070
who removed hundreds of
clearly classified documents

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:23.040
from classified US military
information systems,

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.900
lied to investigators about them,

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:27.990
and then destroyed large
quantities of classified documents.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.940
Contrasting these cases
with the Clinton case,

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:32.781
the FBI highlighted Clinton
received three emails containing

00:24:32.781 --> 00:24:35.010
C-markings for confidential

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:37.620
and that that wasn't clear
evidence of knowledge

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.030
or criminal intent.

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:41.310
As further evidence of
lack of criminal intent,

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:43.350
the FBI highlighted that
Clinton did not originate

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:46.050
this information, but rather
the email chains were forwarded

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:49.350
by staff members with the
confidential markings buried deep

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:51.630
in the email threads.

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:55.080
The DOJ's 2018 report on
Clinton's emails also concluded

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:57.360
that the classification
markings were not clear.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.820
In addition, over 2,000
emails were retroactively

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:02.340
characterized as including
classified information,

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:04.890
which underscores the
uncertainty regarding the status

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:06.330
of Clinton's emails.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.820
In Trump's case, the ex-president
was explicitly notified,

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:11.160
not only that he was
illegally in possession

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:14.220
of highly classified
documents, but the DOJ

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:15.750
and intelligence community had concerns

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:18.300
that those classified items
were not safely stored.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:20.520
According to the Wall Street
Journal, an individual familiar

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:23.010
with the boxes from NARA
had alerted investigators

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:25.170
the documents were not
being stored securely,

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:26.970
which heightened the agency's urgency

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.440
to seize the documents.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:31.290
In the May 10th, 2022 letter,

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:33.870
NARA informed Trump's attorneys
that the boxes recovered

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:36.732
from Mar-a-Lago in January
contained more than 700 pages

00:25:36.732 --> 00:25:38.160
of classified material,

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:40.380
including documents marked,
"extremely sensitive",

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.210
and after reports from inside Mar-a-Lago

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.370
that individuals were seen
moving boxes in and out

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.530
of the room, in June of 2022,

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.360
the DOJ subpoenaed the Trump organization

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.060
for Mar-a-Lago security
footage of the area.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:53.820
Since the search, the government
has recovered more than 300

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:55.830
documents with classified markings,

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:58.080
and according to the
partially unsealed affidavit

00:25:58.080 --> 00:25:59.820
that gave rise to the search warrant,

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.530
NARA referred the matter to the DOJ

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:03.630
for investigation following
a preliminary review

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:05.460
of 15 boxes of documents.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.430
Nara warn the DOJ the classified
documents were haphazardly

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.657
mixed with, quote, "newspapers, magazines,

00:26:10.657 --> 00:26:13.470
"and printed news articles,
among other items."

00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:15.720
Of most significant concern to NARA,

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.480
the classified records
were unfoldered, intermixed

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:22.020
with other records, and
otherwise improperly identified.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:23.580
These fears were confirmed when agents

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.197
who searched Mar-a-Lago found,
quote, "highly sensitive

00:26:26.197 --> 00:26:28.717
"and classified documents
lying in unsecured places

00:26:28.717 --> 00:26:30.607
"outside the storage room
that the Justice Department

00:26:30.607 --> 00:26:33.300
"officials had earlier
asked to be better secured."

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.910
When the FBI conducted a
preliminary review of 15 boxes

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:38.880
that Trump finally turned
over to NARA in May of 2022,

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:40.770
the agency discovered that
Trump was in possession

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:43.327
of at least, quote, "184 unique documents

00:26:43.327 --> 00:26:46.027
"with classified markings,
including 67 documents marked

00:26:46.027 --> 00:26:48.637
"as confidential, 92
documents marked as secret,

00:26:48.637 --> 00:26:50.970
"and 25 documents marked as top secret."

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:52.620
- If you're the president
of the United States,

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:55.620
you can declassify just by
saying it's declassified,

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:57.060
even by thinking about it.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.130
- The affidavit further
alleges that the return boxes

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:01.027
include classified
documents related to, quote,

00:27:01.027 --> 00:27:02.617
"clandestine human sources,

00:27:02.617 --> 00:27:04.357
"who help US intelligence
gathering, as well as

00:27:04.357 --> 00:27:05.767
"how it conducts foreign surveillance

00:27:05.767 --> 00:27:08.610
"and information under lawful
domestic surveillance."

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.160
These are some of America's
most closely held secrets

00:27:11.160 --> 00:27:13.320
and disclosure of this
information could lead

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.510
to sources being compromised or killed,

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:18.120
and the intermingling of personal papers

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.220
with these highly classified documents,

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:23.160
as well as their location
in, what is reported

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:25.560
to be the former
president's personal desk,

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:29.490
all go to state of mind, and
that former President Trump

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:33.000
may have intentionally
kept these documents

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:35.700
and been actively reviewing them long

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:37.560
after they were requested
by the government.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:38.490
- Presidents leave.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:39.323
They take things.

00:27:39.323 --> 00:27:40.230
They take documents,

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:41.063
They take things.

00:27:41.063 --> 00:27:41.896
They read them.

00:27:41.896 --> 00:27:43.440
- Clinton was comparatively
more cooperative

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:45.300
with the DOJs investigation of her server

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.880
than Trump has been with
his classified documents,

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:51.330
which forced the DOJ to be
more aggressive with Trump.

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:52.680
Clinton critics would likely dispute

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:53.670
such a characterization, though,

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.070
noting that 30,000
emails were deleted weeks

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.480
after Clinton received a
congressional subpoena,

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:01.140
though, the deletion was apparently

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.660
by an unidentified server employee.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:05.130
Clinton defenders would counter

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.620
that she did voluntarily turn
over her private email server

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:10.590
to the DOJ in August of 2015,
and the following month,

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:13.320
the FBI was able to recover
many of the deleted emails.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:16.080
Clinton also voluntarily
met with the FBI for a three

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:19.290
and a half hour interview
on July 3rd, 2016.

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:21.570
In contrast, Trump was
vehemently opposed to turning

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.280
over the documents voluntarily,

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:25.860
even in response to a government subpoena,

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:27.870
which is why a search warrant
was apparently required

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:28.980
to get the documents back.

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:30.360
The redacted affidavit alleges

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.700
that NARA began making requests
for the documents return

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.400
in May of 2021, and it
took eight months of delay

00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:37.230
before Team Trump informed NARA

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:39.112
that there were boxes to be recovered.

00:28:39.112 --> 00:28:42.420
On August 16th, 2022, the New
York Times detailed additional

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:44.940
efforts by the FBI to
recover NARA's documents,

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:47.670
and the article highlights
how Trump blew off demands

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.800
to have the documents returned,
telling several advisors,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:51.990
quote, "It's not theirs, it's mine."

